{"id": "gpu-two-adapters-3", "at": "2026-11-26T19:29:50+00:00", "kind": "decision", "stage": 1, "lane": "second-gpu", "text": "Ruling: the adapter query (MeasureGPUInfo) keeps its exact reply format and reports only the main GPU (honoring Main GPU); the telemetry host picks the second GPU itself by the same ordering rule (first other hardware adapter in high-performance order; AdapterInfo.ChooseSecond mirrored in DriverTemperature.cs.txt) | why: TemperatureSuite.lua, OverviewSuite.lua and Run-SettingsSmoke.ps1 anchor the reply as exactly 'OK|...\\nGPU_ADAPTER|1|<luid>' and reject extra lines, so appending the second GPU would change existing assertions | if wrong: add a GPU_SECOND line to the reply and relax those parsers (question-level change to existing tests)"}
{"id": "gpu-two-adapters-4", "at": "2026-11-26T19:29:50+00:00", "kind": "decision", "stage": 1, "lane": "second-gpu", "text": "Ruling: Main GPU reaches the PowerShell command line only through a Calc (MeasureGPUMainChoice, 0/1/2) read as a section variable, so MeasureGPUInfo gains DynamicVariables=1 | why: saved settings text is never pasted into a PowerShell command | if wrong: one include variant per choice instead"}
{"id": "gpu-two-adapters-5", "at": "2026-11-26T19:38:57+00:00", "kind": "decision", "stage": 1, "lane": "second-gpu", "text": "Ruling: the DXCore path applies to any discrete adapter whose NVIDIA open reports UNSUPPORTED (no NVAPI, or the LUID is not an NVIDIA logical GPU), so an NVIDIA card without a working NVAPI also reads DXCore load and memory; a bound NVIDIA driver that fails at runtime stays UNAVAILABLE with no fallback (today's rule) | why: the approved rule is 'anything non-NVIDIA reads through DXCore'; keying on driver binding rather than vendor avoids a vendor-name shortcut, which the module forbids | if wrong: key on the PCI vendor ID instead"}
{"id": "gpu-two-adapters-6", "at": "2026-11-26T19:38:58+00:00", "kind": "decision", "stage": 1, "lane": "second-gpu", "text": "Ruling: second-GPU samples go to a sibling session file <session>.second (GPU_SECOND|1, 14 fields) rather than new fields in the main snapshot | why: TemperatureSuite.lua requires exactly 27 fields and protocol 4 in the main snapshot | if wrong: bump the main protocol to 5 (question-level change to TemperatureSuite)"}
{"id": "gpu-two-adapters-7", "at": "2026-11-26T19:41:01+00:00", "kind": "question", "stage": 1, "lane": "second-gpu", "text": "M3 must change two existing Validate-GPU.py expectations. (1) Line 385 'assert sections[\"Rainmeter\"][\"DynamicWindowSize\"] == \"0\"': the second section appears only once the running host reports a second hardware GPU (single-GPU machines must keep today's height), so the panel and window grow at runtime; GPU.ini goes to DynamicWindowSize=1. (2) Lines 518-519 'assert set(meters) == EXPECTED_METERS' and 'assert len(meters) == (61 if MEMORY else 55 if TABLE else 41)': the section adds meters (second-GPU name/load row, Video row with bar, memory row, History overlay trace). My change keeps the 61-meter base set and its count assertion (line 510) intact and adds a separate SECOND_METERS set (always loaded; zero height and hidden until a second GPU is reported), so lines 518-519 compare against base + second. All other geometry assertions run unchanged with the section collapsed (GPUSecondBlock=0), and new checks of the section's expanded geometry are added. If you reject this, the alternative is a load-time decision (e.g. writing a detected 'two GPUs' flag into User\\GPU.inc and refreshing once), which avoids DynamicWindowSize but still adds meters."}
{"id": "gpu-two-adapters-8", "at": "2026-11-26T19:46:26+00:00", "kind": "notice", "stage": 1, "text": "Power session (local_9be49795) reports NVAPI identity/temperature/memory and NVML power wake the RTX from D3 (its measurements, not re-run here); ReadLoadFrame would too. It says David approved a D-state gate there. Agreed order: lane finishes first, it builds on goal/gpu-two-adapters afterwards; the gate is not in this goal's scope."}
{"id": "gpu-two-adapters-9", "at": "2026-11-26T19:47:08+00:00", "kind": "answer", "stage": 1, "answers": "gpu-two-adapters-7", "text": "David: yes, allow. Validate-GPU.py may set DynamicWindowSize=1 and add the 8 second-GPU meters as their own expected set; the existing base assertions stay."}
{"id": "gpu-two-adapters-10", "at": "2026-11-26T19:58:32+00:00", "kind": "question", "stage": 1, "lane": "second-gpu", "text": "M4 (GPU Settings offers Main GPU and Second GPU) must change four existing settings-test expectations. Design: two stepper rows in the Display section, the same 5-meter pattern as Meter width (label, <, value frame, value, >), shifting everything below by 56 logical px. Affected: (1) tests/Validate-Settings.py line 43 'assert len(meters) == 65' -> 75; (2) Validate-Settings.py line 125 'assert math.isclose(n(\"#PanelHeight#\"), 704 if table == \"1\" else 676)' -> 760 / 732; (3) tests/Run-SettingsSmoke.ps1 line 621 and (4) line 678, both matching '^PanelHeight=704' in settings-observed.txt -> 760. Validate-Settings' generic geometry checks (no overlap, inside window) stay as they are and must pass with the new rows; new checks for the two rows and SettingsSuite.lua cases for cycling and saving GPUMainGPU / GPUSecondGPU are added. Alternative that needs no test change: leave the two choices as documented Advanced (User\\GPU.inc) keys only, which does not meet M4 as planned. Meanwhile I am writing docs and running the native check of M1-M3."}
{"id": "gpu-two-adapters-11", "at": "2026-11-26T20:03:31+00:00", "kind": "question", "stage": 1, "lane": "second-gpu", "text": "Correction to my previous M4 settings question (exact list after reading tests/Validate-Settings.py in full). Design now: two click-to-cycle value rows (label + value, the Process table pattern, 4 meters), 'Main GPU' (Auto/Integrated/Discrete) and 'Second GPU' (Off/One line/Rows/Rows + history), in the Display section right after Process VRAM, 28 px each. Existing expectations that must change: Validate-Settings.py line 21 GPUSettingsShift == '(28+28*#GPUSettingsVRAMRow#)' -> '(84+28*#GPUSettingsVRAMRow#)'; line 43 len(meters) == 65 -> 69; line 125 PanelHeight 704/676 -> 760/732; line 145 the Provider setup heading's distance is measured from the last shown Display row (shown[-1]) -> measured from the new last row 'SecondGPU' (same 34 px distance); Run-SettingsSmoke.ps1 lines 621 and 678 '^PanelHeight=704' -> 760. Nothing else in existing tests changes; new spacing/wiring checks for the two rows and SettingsSuite cases are added. Awaiting yes/no; implementing the rows meanwhile without touching those assertions."}
